## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 0133509772c96703549939c1db7fbc7c64123578

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I ran `git log -p --follow` on `ConventionJdt.java` to see the full history of the file leading up to the fix commit `a9a3e6c`. This showed a direct parent commit `0133509` that refactored the `compilerComplianceLevel` method and the `close()` logic from using dedicated `Action` inner classes (`JavaComplianceAction`, `JavaClasspathVariableAction`) into inline lambdas registered eagerly via `extension.workspaceProp(...)`.

**(2) The specific change:** Before commit `0133509`, the code was:
```java
public void compilerComplianceLevel(String compilerComplianceLevel) {
    this.compilerComplianceLevel = JavaVersion.toVersion(compilerComplianceLevel);
}
...
JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, complianceLevel.toString())); // complianceLevel is a JavaVersion
```
The raw string parameter was converted to a `JavaVersion` via `JavaVersion.toVersion(...)` and only the normalized `JavaVersion.toString()` was ever stored into the properties.

In commit `0133509`, this was refactored into:
```java
public void compilerComplianceLevel(String compilerComplianceLevel) {
    ...
    extension.workspaceProp(JDT_CORE_PREFS, props -> {
        JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, compilerComplianceLevel.toString()));
        ...
    });
}
```
Here `compilerComplianceLevel` inside the lambda refers to the raw `String` method parameter (shadowing), so `.toString()` is just an identity no-op on the String — the `JavaVersion.toVersion(...)` normalization step was dropped entirely. This changes behavior for any non-canonical input (e.g. `"6"` vs `"1.6"`), since the raw string is now written straight into the JDT prefs instead of the version-normalized form.

**(3) Evidence:** The fix commit `a9a3e6c` reinstates exactly this missing conversion:
```java
-JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, compilerComplianceLevel.toString()));
+JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, JavaVersion.toVersion(compilerComplianceLevel).toString()));
```
This is a precise, surgical restoration of the normalization logic that was present before `0133509` and lost during that refactor — confirming `0133509` is where the regression was introduced. No later commits touch this line before the fix, so I'm confident in this single bug-introducing commit.

